# Activatable nanoscale metal-organic framework for ratiometric photoacoustic imaging of hydrogen sulfide and orthotopic colorectal cancer in vivo

1College of Chemical Engineering, Fuzhou University, Fuzhou 350116, China; 2Key Laboratory for Analytical Science of Food Safety and Biology, Ministry of Education, College of Chemistry, Fuzhou University, Fuzhou 350116, China; 3Laboratory of Molecular Imaging and Nanomedicine, National Institute of Biomedical Imaging and Bioengineering, National Institutes of Health, Bethesda, MD 20892, USA
eceived May 10, 2020; accepted May 15, 2020; published online June 15, 2020
Nanoscale metal-organic frameworks (nano MOFs) have emerged as a promising biomedical nanoplatform because of their unique properties. However, the exploration of nano MOFs in photoacoustic (PA) imaging is still limited. Here, a novel hydrogen sulfide $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{S)}$ -activated nano copper-based MOF (Cu-MOF) was developed as a near-infrared (NIR) ratiometric PA probe for in vivo monitoring of endogenous $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ level and orthotopic colorectal cancer imaging via in situ reaction of nano Cu-MOFs with endogenous $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ that is closely associated with tumor growth and proliferation in colon cancer. The synthesized nano $\mathtt{C u}$ -MOFs displayed excellent PA responsiveness towards tumor $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ level with high selectivity and rapid kinetics. The result suggests the developed probe may provide a unique opportunity to investigate the malignant behaviors of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ -associated events in vivo.
PA imaging, MOFs, ratiometric probe, $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{S}$ , orthotopic colorectal cancer
Citation: Zhang W, Wang J, Su L, Chen H, Zhang L, Lin L, Chen X, Song J, Yang H. Activatable nanoscale metal-organic framework for ratiometric photoacoustic imaging of hydrogen sulfide and orthotopic colorectal cancer in vivo. Sc China Chem, 2020, 63, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11426-020- 9775-y
## 1 Introduction
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are an important class of porous materials self-assembled from metal ions with organic ligands through coordination bonds, which have been widely used for gas storage and separation, catalysis, chemical sensing, drug delivery, etc. [1–6], because of their high surface area and porosity, structural and compositional tunability, multifunctionality, and accessible metal active sites. In the past decade, through merging nanotechnology and MOFs, nanoscale MOFs (nano MOFs) are emerging as a new nanomaterial platform with unique opportunities for biomedical applications due to their improved biocompatibility [7,8]. In particular for bioimaging, the noticeable advance of applications of nano MOFs has been made in recent years [9], however, the exploration of nano MOFs in photoacoustic (PA) imaging is still limited [10,11].
PA imaging is a very powerful imaging modality that detects generated ultrasonic pressure waves induced by optical excitation (known as the PA effect) [12,13]. This PA effect endows PA imaging with high spatial resolution and deep tissue penetration depth [12]. Nevertheless, PA imaging of numerous physiological and disease processes usually suffers from the serious light scattering of biological tissues as well as undesirable nonspecific signal output, leading to the insufficient sensitivity of PA imaging [14–16]. Thus, the development of functional PA probes, especially ratiometric probes with near-infrared (NIR, $650{-}1,700\mathrm{nm}$ light absorption, is highly required to improve the signal to noise ratio of PA imaging [17–22]. Ratiometric PA probes can not only offer real-time information upon molecular targetassociated biological and pathological status at molecular level with low background noise, but also significantly eliminate the interference arising from in vivo heterogeneous probe distribution and tissue scattering. Moreover, NIR lightabsorbing property of PA probes also can alleviate the light scattering effect of tissues [23], which further improves the penetration depth and enhances the signal to noise ratio of PA imaging. Considering the advantages of MOFs described above, nano MOFs would be promising candidates as ratiometric PA probes for imaging specific physiological species in the NIR spectral region. Specifically, because of their highly tunable structure and composition, nano MOFs with NIR light absorption can be constructed by NIR lightabsorbing organic ligands or metal ions [24,25]. In addition, MOFs have abundant accessible metal ions and organic ligands, both of which can be used as active sites for preparation of ratiometric probes [26–28]. Furthermore, the large surface area and high porosity render nano MOFs faster chemical kinetics than common organic/inorganic nanoparticles [29,30], facilitating the real-time bioimaging of molecular targets. However, to our knowledge, the study of nano MOFs as NIR ratiometric PA probes has not been reported. Additionally, since the thermodynamics difference of MOF formation for various metal-ligand combinations, synthetic methods must be carefully tailored for each specific framework of nano MOFs.
Herein, we report the synthesis of uniform nano copperbased MOFs (Cu-MOFs, known as HKUST-1) using a solvothermal method combined with both steric stabilization and coordination modulation strategy, in which polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP), an amphiphilic polymer, was used not only as a modulator to provide steric stabilization as well as regulate crystallization of nano Cu-MOFs through coordination between pyrrolidone groups and ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ , also as a protecting ligand to improve the stability and biocompatibility of nano MOFs. Furthermore, the synthesized PVPmodified nano $\mathrm{Cu}$ -MOF served as a ratiometric PA probe for in vivo monitoring of endogenous hydrogen sulfide $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{S)}$ level and orthotopic colorectal cancer imaging in NIR window via in situ reaction of nano Cu-MOF with endogenous $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ to generate copper sulfide, as shown in Scheme 1. $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ , as an endogenous gasotransmitter, is closely associated with tumor growth and proliferation in colon cancer with concentration range of $0.3{-}3.4\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ [31–35]. Thus, realtime in vivo detection of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ is invaluable for understanding the onset and progression of colon cancer.
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Materials
PVP (average $M_{\mathrm{W}}~10{,}000)$ , copper(II) nitrate trihydrate $(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ $299\%$ , sodium hydrosulfide hydrate $\mathrm{(NaHS\cdot{xH_{2}O})}$ , glycine (Gly, $298\%$ ), L-cysteine (Cys, $\geq97\%)$ , L-glutathione reduced (GSH, $298\%$ and hydrogen peroxide solution $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , $3\mathrm{wt\%}$ in $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{\cdot}^{\cdot}$ were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich (USA). 1,3,5-Benzenetricarboxylic acid (BTC, $295\%$ was obtained from J&K Scientific Ltd. (China). $S.$ -adenosyl-L-methionine (SAM, $298\%$ ) and aminooxyacetic acid (AOAA, $298\%$ ) were purchased from Macklin Biochemical Co., Ltd. (China). Sodium dihydrogen phosphate anhydrous $(\geq99\%)$ and sodium phosphate dibasic dodecahydrate $(\geq99\%)$ were purchased from Aladdin Chemistry Co., Ltd. (China). Ethanol (EtOH, $299.7\%$ ), N,Ndimethylformamide (DMF, $299.5\%$ , sodium chloride $(\geq99.5\%)$ , sodium carbonate $(\geq99.8\%)$ , sodium sulfate $(\geq99\%)$ , sodium acetate $(\geq99\%)$ , sodium thiosulfate $(\geq99\%)$ and sodium fluoride $({\geq}98\%)$ were purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. (China). All chemicals were used directly without further purification.
## 2.2 Instrument
Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) and element mapping images were obtained using a HT7700 Exalens (Hitachi, Japan) and a Talos $\mathrm{F}200~\mathrm{G}2\$ (FEI, USA), respectively. Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) analyses were performed on a Helios G4 CX (Thermo Fisher, USA). UVVis-NIR absorption spectra were recorded using an UH415 spectrophotometer (Hitachi, Japan). X-ray diffraction (XRD)
patterns were collected on an Ultima IV 285 X-ray powder diffractometer (Rigaku Co., Japan). Fourier-transform infrared (FT-IR) spectra were measured on a Nicolet is50 spectrometer (Thermo Fisher, USA). Surface area measurements were carried out on an ASAP 2020 Analyzer (Micromeritics, USA). Bioluminescence and PA imaging of tumor-bearing mice were performed on an Amix small animal imaging system (Spectral Instruments Imaging, USA), and a Vevo 3100 small animal in vivo imaging system (Fuji Film, Canada), respectively.
## 2.3 Synthesis of nano Cu-MOFs
## 2.4 In vitro ratiometric response behavior of nano Cu-MOFs to NaHS
In a typical experiment, $6{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ of NaSH solution with different concentrations (0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1.0, 1.5 and $2.0\mathrm{mM}$ ) was added into $600~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of Tris-HCl buffer $\mathrm{(pH~7.4)}$ containing nano Cu-MOFs $(0.5\mathrm{\mg\mL}^{-1})_{\mathrm{\ell}}$ ), respectively. After incubated at room temperature for $2\mathrm{min}$ , the obtained mixtures were measured by UV-Vis-NIR absorption spectrophotometer. For ratiometric PA imaging, the obtained mixtures were imaged under excitation at 700 and $1250\mathrm{nm}$ using a Vevo 3100 small animal in vivo imaging system. For the anti-interference ability in different pH buffer, the Tris-HCl buffer (pH 7.4) was replaced by Tris-HCl buffer $\mathrm{\pH}8.2$ and 9.0) and citrate buffer $\mathrm{\bar{pH}}5.0$ and 6.2), respectively. For the antiinterference ability against biochemical species, the Tris-HCl buffers $\mathrm{(pH}7.4)$ with different biochemical species $(2.0\mathrm{mM})$ were used.
## 2.5 Cell culture
Human normal embryo liver cell line L02, human colon cancer cell line HT-29 and murine colorectal cancer cell line MC38-Luc were obtained from the Cell Bank of the Chinese Academy of Sciences. The L02, HT-29 and MC38-Luc cells were cultured in Dulbecco’s modified Eagle medium (DMEM, Gibco) supplemented with $10\%$ fetal bovine serum (FBS, Gibco), and incubated at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in a humidified atmosphere with $5\%\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ .
## 2.6 In vitro cytotoxicity assay
The cytotoxicity of nano Cu-MOFs was measured using standard cell kit-8 (CCK-8) assay. In brief, the L02 and HT-29 cells were seeded in 96-well plates and incubated overnight, and subsequently incubated with $100\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of varying concentrations of nano $\mathrm{Cu}$ -MOFs $(0-100\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{mL}^{-1},$ for another $24\mathrm{h}$ . The cells were then washed with Tris-HCl buffer $\mathrm{(pH~7.4)}$ three times. Cell viabilities were measured according to the manufacturer’s protocol of CCK-8 assay.
## 2.7 Mice tumor model construction
All animal experiments obeyed the Guidelines set by the Care and Use of Laboratory Animals (Ministry of Science and Technology of China, 2006) and were approved by the Institutional Animal Care and Use Committee of Fujian Medical University. For subcutaneous tumor model construction, the tumor-bearing BALB/C nude mice with an average tumor volume of ${\sim}100\mathrm{mm}^{3}$ were achieved by hypodermic inoculation of $100~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of HT-29 cells $(10^{6})$ in the right hind leg of mice. For orthotopic tumor model construction, $25{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ of MC38-Luc cells $(10^{7})$ was inoculated into the cecum serosa of C57BL/6 black mice that fasted for $12\mathrm{h}$ before surgery. The orthotopic tumor growth was monitored by an Amix small animal imaging system through intraperitoneal injection with luciferin ( $30\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ , $100~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ .
## 2.8 In vivo PA imaging of tumor-bearing mice
The mice were anesthetized using $1\%-2\%$ isoflurane inhalation in air during in vivo photoacoustic imaging. For subcutaneous tumor PA imaging, the HT-29 tumor-bearing mice models were randomly divided into four groups, including only Cu-MOFs injection, Cu-MOFs injection post SAM pretreatment $(\mathrm{Cu-MOFs+SAM})$ , Cu-MOFs injection post AOAA pretreatment $(\mathrm{Cu-MOFs+AOAA})$ , and phosphate buffered saline (PBS) injection groups, where SAM $(20\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{kg}^{-1})$ and AOAA $(2\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{kg}^{-1})$ were utilized to upregulate and suppress endogenous $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ expression, respectively, and both of them were intraperitoneally injected $12\mathrm{h}$ before the probe injection. The ratiometric PA images were collected under excitation at 700 and $1{,}250\mathrm{nm}$ after intravenous injection of $200~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of nano Cu-MOFs $(10\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}),$ .
For orthotopic tumor PA imaging, the mice were fasted $12\mathrm{{h}}$ before intravenous injection of $200\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of nano CuMOFs $(20\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{mL}^{-1})$ . The orthotopic tumor-bearing mice were imaged at both wavelengths of 700 and $1,250\mathrm{nm}$ .
## 2.9 Ex vivo bioluminescence imaging of colon tumor
As the PA imaging experiment was completed, the orthotopic tumor-bearing mice were sacrificed at $5\mathrm{{min}}$ post intraperitoneal injection with luciferin $(30\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ , $100~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ for the ex vivo bioluminescence imaging of colon tumor. The tumor was then carefully dissected and rinsed with PBS buffer several times, and immediately imaged with an Amix small animal imaging system.
## 3 Results and discussion
## 3.1 Synthesis and characterization of $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{S}$ -activated nano Cu-MOFs
To obtain well-defined nano Cu-MOFs with uniform size, we first investigated the effect of reaction solvent on crystallization of nano Cu-MOFs. As shown in Figures S1 and S2 (Supporting Information online), regardless of whether DMF or EtOH (common solvents for MOFs synthesis) was used as reaction solvent, both the obtained samples had an average size of ${\sim}77.9\ \mathrm{nm}$ . However, when DMF was used as reaction solvent, the resulting nanocrystals became heterogeneous with a coefficient of variation (CV) of ${\sim}66\%$ but highly defined, while the choice of EtOH as reaction solvent provided highly fused but relatively homogeneous $(\mathrm{CV=}\sim23\%$ nanocrystals. Considering the above results, the feasibility of DMF/EtOH mixtures (DMF/EtOH $:=5$ , 2, 0.5 and 0.2) as reaction solvent was also investigated (Figures S3 and S4). The results showed the nanocrystals became more uniform with decreasing ratio of DMF/EtOH, meanwhile, all of them had similar average particle sizes, suggesting high content of EtOH contributes to temporally separate crystal nucleation from crystal growth to obtain homogeneous nano Cu-MOFs because of the change of the coordination environment of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ [36,37]. Additionally, in our design, PVP not only plays a significant role in controlling the crystal size, but also serves as a protecting ligand to improve the stability and biocompatibility of nanocrystals. As shown in Figures S5 and S6, through altering the PVP concentrations, distinct nanocrystal sizes can be achieved in the range of $45{-}150~\mathrm{nm}$ , all of which exhibited a clear octahedron structure. The higher PVP concentrations, the smaller the crystal size obtained, indicating that PVP could effectively suppress the crystal growth of nano $\mathrm{Cu}$ -MOFs through weak coordination between pyrrolidone group of PVP and ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ [38]. At the same time, PVP gave the nano Cu-MOFs good stability and biocompatibility (Figures S7–S9).
In a typical synthesis of nano Cu-MOF, an optimal amount of PVP was added into the mixed solvent of DMF/EtOH containing $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and BTC. The mixture was ultrasonicated for $30\mathrm{min}$ , and heated at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $8\mathrm{h}$ in an autoclave. The resulted blue-green solid was collected by centrifugation and washed several times with EtOH for subsequent characterization. As shown in Figure 1(a–c), the obtained nano $\mathrm{Cu}$ -MOFs displayed a well-defined octahedron, and had an average diameter of ${\sim}92.9\mathrm{nm}$ with a CV of ${\sim}17.8\%$ . The Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface areas and total pore volume of the nano Cu-MOFs were calculated to be $90\bar{0}.1\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ and $0.672{\mathrm{~cm}}^{3}{\mathrm{~g}}^{-1}$ (Figure 1(d)). XRD data showed the reflection peaks of the nano Cu-MOFs matched well with those of simulated octahedron crystals of HKUST-1 (Figure 1(e)). Besides, in the FT-IR spectra of the obtained samples (Figure 1(f)), the typical peaks at 1,446 and $1{,}371~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were assigned to the symmetric stretching vibrations of the $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{COO}}^{-}$ group in BTC, while the characteristic peaks at 1,645 and $1{,}589\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ attributed to both the asymmetric stretching vibrations of the $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{coo}^{-}}$ group in BTC and the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ stretching of the amide unit in PVP. The peak at $1{,}292\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ also corresponded to the $-C-N_{-}$ vibrations of the amine group in PVP. These above results confirmed the successful preparation of PVP-modified nano $\mathrm{Cu}$ -MOF.
## 3.2 In vitro sulfuration reaction of nano Cu-MOFs
Subsequently, NaHS was employed as a $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ donor for investigating the sulfuration reaction of nano Cu-MOFs (Figure 2(a)). As shown in Figure $2(\mathtt{b},\mathtt{c})$ , after reacting nano CuMOFs with NaHS, the spherical nanoparticles with an average size of ${\sim}88.3\ \mathrm{nm}$ were achieved. The XRD and Xray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) analyses revealed that the resulted nanoparticles were rhombohedral copper sulfide crystals $\mathrm{(Cu_{9}S_{5}}$ , JCPDS card No. 47-1748) (Figure 2(d) and Figure S10). The clear interplanar $d$ -spacing of ${\sim}0.35\mathrm{nm}$ in the high-solution TEM (HR-TEM) image of the obtained sample corresponded to the (1,0,1) lattice fringe of rhombohedral $\mathrm{Cu}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{5}$ crystals (Figure 2(e)), further suggesting the formation of copper sulfide nanoparticles. Additionally, detailed information on the composition of the nano Cu-MOFs before and after reacting with NaHS was also investigated with elemental mapping. As shown in Figure 2(f, g), compared with relative element content of the nano Cu-MOFs, the relative sulfur content of the obtained nanoparticles increased obviously, while the slightly decrease of the relative content of both carbon and oxygen was observed, indicating that the generation of copper sulfide nanoparticles could be achieved by replacing the BTC ligand in the nano Cu-MOFs with NaHS because of the different coordination abilities with ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ between $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{coo}^{-}}$ group in BTC and $\mathrm{HS}^{-}$ . It was also found that PVP ligands were still coated on the surface of copper sulfide nanoparticles, which was supported by no obvious variation of the relative nitrogen content (amide unit in PVP) as well as FT-IR spetrum (Figure S11). This was due to the fact that most of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ were confined into the steric structure provided by PVP through coordination between pyrrolidone groups and ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ during the fomation of copper sulfide nanoparticles. Collectively, the results demonstrated that nano Cu-MOF can react with NaHS to generate copper sulfide. Besides, the obtained copper sulfide nanoparticles also displayed sufficient stability in physiological media (Figure S12).
As well known, a prerequisite for ratiometric PA detection and imaging is the relative absorption change of one wavelength to another wavelength before and after the responsive reaction. In Figure 2(h), the nano Cu-MOFs showed strong absorption in NIR-I $(650-1,000\ \mathrm{nm})$ spectral region with a maximum absorption peak at $700\mathrm{nm}$ , whereas, after adding NaHS into the nano Cu-MOFs solution, the absorption intensity in NIR-I window of the mixture decreased greatly and the absorption capability in NIR-II window (1,000– $1,700\mathrm{nm})$ was significantly enhanced, which attributes to both the different band gaps of the two nanoparticles and the localized surface plasmon resonances (LSPR) effect of copper sulfide [39,40]. The result implied the potential of the nano Cu-MOFs as a ratiometric probe. Furthermore, the antiinterference ability of nano Cu-MOFs reacting with NaHS was investigated. As shown in Figure 2(i), the absorption ratio $\mathrm{(Abs_{1250}/A b s_{700})}$ obtained from the sulfuration reaction in different $\mathsf{p H}$ buffers showed no obvious $\mathsf{p H}$ dependence manner, indicating that the $\mathsf{p H}$ of physiological and pathological environment had no significant effect on the response of nano ${\mathrm{Cu-MOFs}}$ to NaHS. The sulfuration reaction also exhibited good anti-interference ability against various biochemical species, and even biothiols (Cys and GSH, $2.0\mathrm{mM})$ (Figure 2(j)).
To examine the response behavior of the nano Cu-MOFs to the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ level in vitro, the absorption spectra were then recorded. As shown in Figure 3(a) and Figure S13, the addition of different concentrations of NaHS resulted in a drop of absorption in NIR-I spectral region and obvious absorption enhancement in NIR-II window. The conversion of nano $\mathrm{Cu}$ -MOFs after reaction with $2.0\mathrm{mM}\mathrm{NaHS}$ was estimated to be ${\sim}64\%$ (Figure S14). For PA imaging, the changes of PA signals exhibited the same trends as that of the absorption spectra (Figure 3(b)). Subsequently, a good linear relationship $(R^{2}{=}0.98)$ between the PA signal ratio $\mathrm{(PA_{1250}/P A_{700})}$ and the concentration of NaHS range of $0.25{-}2.0\mathrm{mM}$ was obtained with the limit of detection of $0.1\mathrm{mM}$ (Figure 3(c)). The kinetics of the sulfuration reaction was also investigated, revealing that the nano Cu-MOFs reacted with NaHS quickly and reached chemical equilibrium within $30~\mathrm{s}$ (Figure 3(d)
and Figure S15). These results suggested the possibility of the nano Cu-MOFs as a NIR ratiometric PA probe for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ detection and colon cancer imaging.
Subsequently, we evaluated the capability of the nano CuMOFs for ratiometric PA imaging of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ in living cells. As shown in Figure S16, when HT-29 cells were pretreated with $\mathrm{ZnCl}_{2}$ to scavenge endogenous $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ , the $\mathrm{PA}_{1250}/\mathrm{PA}_{700}$ in nano Cu-MOFs treated cell pellets reduced substantially. Once the cells were further incubated with NaHS, a large $\mathrm{PA}_{1250}/\mathrm{PA}_{700}$ enhancement was observed. Besides, after being treated with L-Cys (a precursor for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ biosynthesis) to stimulate the endogenous $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ expression in HT-29 cells, strong $\mathrm{PA}_{1250}/$ $\mathrm{PA}_{700}$ in the cells was also obtained, which was greatly inhibited when pretreated with AOAA $\mathrm{{\cdot}}\mathrm{{{H}}}_{2}\mathrm{{{S}}}}$ -synthetase inhibitor). These results further confirmed the potential of the nano Cu-MOFs for ratiometric PA imaging of endogenous $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ levels in colon cancer.
## 3.3 In vivo PA imaging of $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{S}$ in subcutaneous tumor
In the following animal experiments, the mice bearing subcutaneous HT-29 tumor xenografts were used to further evaluate the in vivo ratiometric responsiveness of the nano $\mathrm{Cu}$ -MOFs to endogenous $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ . These tumor-bearing mice were randomly divided into four groups, including only CuMOFs injection, Cu-MOFs injection post SAM pretreatment $(\mathrm{Cu-MOFs+SAM}_{,}$ , Cu-MOFs injection post AOAA pretreatment $(\mathrm{Cu-MOFs+AOAA}^{}$ ), and PBS injection groups, where SAM and AOAA were utilized to upregulate and suppress endogenous $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ expression, respectively [31]. The PA images of all the groups were collected at both wavelengths of 700 and $1,250\mathrm{nm}$ . Because of the intrinsic weak
PA signals of tumors, the PA signal increment ΔPA (obtained by subtracting the PA signal of tissue before probes injection from the generated PA signal after probes injection) was determined to present the ratiometric behavior of the PA probes. As shown in Figure $4(\mathsf{a-c})$ and Figure S17, because of the enhanced permeability and retention (EPR) effect of the nanoprobe, both $\Delta\mathrm{PA}_{1250}$ and $\Delta\mathrm{PA}_{700}$ in the three groups (Cu-MOFs, $\mathrm{Cu-MOFs+SAM}$ , $\mathrm{Cu-MOFs+AOAA}$ ) gradually increased over time and reached the maximum at $12\mathrm{h}$ post intravenous injection of the nano $\mathrm{Cu}$ -MOFs. In particular, the ΔPA at 1,250 nm followed the order of ΔPA(Cu-MOF+SAM) > $\Delta\mathrm{PA}_{\mathrm{(Cu-MOF)}}>\Delta\mathrm{PA}_{\mathrm{(Cu-MOF+AOAA)}}.$ , while there was an opposite order observed at the wavelength of $700\mathrm{nm}$ : $\Delta\mathrm{PA}_{\mathrm{(Cu-MOF+SAM)}}$ $<\Delta\mathrm{PA}_{\mathrm{(Cu-MOF)}}<\Delta\mathrm{PA}_{\mathrm{(Cu-MOF+AOAA)}}$ , indicating that the outstanding ratiometric response behavior of the nano-MOFs to different endogenous $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ levels in vivo. More importantly, the ratio of the PA signal increment $(\Delta\mathrm{PA}_{1250}/\Delta\mathrm{PA}_{700})$ maintained steadily from 12 to $24\mathrm{h}$ after an initial increase, suggesting that the amount of the nano $\mathrm{Cu}$ -MOFs at $12\mathrm{h}$ post injection was sufficient to reflect the level of endogenous $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ within tumors. Besides, the $\Delta\mathrm{PA}_{1250}/\Delta\mathrm{PA}_{700}$ can be enhanced and reduced by the SAM (activator) and AOAA (inhibitor) respectively, as well as displayed a ${\sim}13$ -fold difference between HT-29 tumor and MCF-7 tumor $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{-}$ deficient) at $12\mathrm{h}$ post injection of nano Cu-MOFs (Figure 4 (d) and Figure S18). These results demonstrated that the nano Cu-MOFs is a promising ratiometric PA probe for in vivo monitoring of endogenous $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ expression level and colon cancer imaging.
## 3.4 In vivo PA imaging of $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{S}$ in orthotopic tumor
Considering the above inspiring results and the deep tissue penetration of NIR light, the nano Cu-MOFs were further used for ratiometric PA imaging of the mice bearing orthotopic colorectal cancer that is a more valuable model to study the biology and pathology of human colon cancer than the subcutaneous colon cancer model. The images of in vivo bioluminescence and ultrasonic (US) imaging of orthotopic tumor-bearing mice as well as ex vivo bioluminescence of dissected tumor demonstrated the successful establishment of orthotopic colorectal cancer (Figure 5(a–c)). Similarly, the PA images were collected under excitation at 700 and $1,250\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively, and the corresponding PA signal increment ΔPA was used to evaluate the capability of the nano ${\mathrm{Cu-MOFs}}$ for ratiometric PA imaging of orthotopic colorectal tumor. After intravenous injection of the nano Cu-MOFs, both $\Delta\mathrm{PA}_{1250}$ and $\Delta\mathrm{PA}_{700}$ significant increased due to the continuous accumulation of the nano $\mathrm{Cu}$ -MOFs at the tumor site, and reached the maximum at $12\mathrm{h}$ post injection (Figure 5(d, e)). Furthermore, the $\Delta\mathrm{PA}_{1250}/\Delta\mathrm{PA}_{700}$ reached a plateau at $12\mathrm{h}$ post injection after an initial gradually increase, and the $\Delta\mathrm{PA}_{1250}/\Delta\mathrm{PA}_{700}$ at $12\mathrm{{h}}$ post injection $(0.52{\scriptstyle\pm0.07})$ was ${\sim}6.5$ -fold higher than that at 0.5 post injection $(0.08{\scriptstyle\pm0.03})$ (Figure 5(f)). Finally, inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry (ICP-MS) was used to evaluate the possible clearance pathway of the nano CuMOFs by analyzing the mice feces and urine collected at different time points after administration. As shown in Figure S19, nano Cu-MOFs could be cleared out through feces after $14{\mathrm{~d~}}$ post injection along with little change in the body weight of the mice. These results provided strong evidence for the potential application of the nano Cu-MOFs for PA imaging of colon cancer.
## 4 Conclusions
In conclusion, a novel $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ -activated nano Cu-MOF was developed as a NIR ratiometric PA probe for in vivo monitoring of endogenous $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ level and orthotopic colorectal cancer imaging via in situ reaction of the nano Cu-MOFs with endogenous $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ to generate copper sulfide nanoparticles. The nano $\mathrm{Cu-MOF}$ was prepared by the solvothermal method, where PVP was added not only as a modulator to control the crystal size and also as a protecting ligand to improve the stability and biocompatibility of nanocrystals. More importantly, the synthesized nano Cu-MOFs exhibited outstanding ratiometric PA responsiveness toward endogenous $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ level in orthotopic tumor with high selectivity and fast kinetics. This study presents the feasibility of nano $\mathrm{Cu}$ -based MOFs for ratiometric PA imaging of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ , which may provide a great opportunity to explore the malignant behaviors of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ -associated events in vivo.
